<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Mainland Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Cambodia (1953-1970)</h1><p class="st-meta">1953 CE – 1970 CE · capital Phnom Penh</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Independent Cambodia under Norodom Sihanouk, who kept the kingdom neutral and peaceful through the 1950s and 1960s while war raged next door.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norodom-of-cambodia">King Norodom</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norodom-sihanouk">Sihanouk</a>'s campaign against French rule won full independence on 9 November 1953, confirmed by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/republic-of-geneva">Geneva</a> conference in 1954. In 1955 he abdicated in favour of his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norodom-suramarit">Norodom Suramarit</a> to enter politics, founding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sangkum-reastr-niyum">Sangkum Reastr Niyum</a> movement, which won every seat in the national assembly.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norodom-sihanouk">Sihanouk</a> dominated politics as prime minister and, after his father's death in 1960, head of state. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sangkum-reastr-niyum">Sangkum</a> promoted Buddhist socialism, built schools, hospitals and modern buildings in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phnom-penh">Phnom Penh</a>, and pursued neutrality in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a>. In 1965 Sihanouk broke with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-states">United States</a> and tacitly allowed North Vietnamese forces to use Cambodian territory, while repressing the left.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>A deteriorating economy, a peasant revolt at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samlaut-uprising">Samlaut</a> in 1967 and growing hostility from the right weakened him. On 18 March 1970, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norodom-sihanouk">Sihanouk</a> was abroad, the national assembly deposed him, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lon-nol">Lon Nol</a> proclaimed the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/khmer-republic">Khmer Republic</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 1953 CE – 1970 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Mainland Southeast Asia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:98.57%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1953 CE – 1970 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Phnom Penh</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Norodom Sihanouk, Norodom Suramarit</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Theravada Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Khmer</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/cambodia">Cambodia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Cambodia, Sangkum era Cambodia</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1962&amp;focus=kingdom-of-cambodia-1953-1970" aria-label="Kingdom of Cambodia (1953-1970) on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7566 1746 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Cambodia (1953-1970) lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7886 2002L7886 2002M7886 2002L7886 2002"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7886 2002h0M7886 2002h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7886 2002h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7886 2002h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Phnom Penh. <a href="/atlas?year=1962&amp;focus=kingdom-of-cambodia-1953-1970">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
